      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/funkley_minnesota",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Funkley's early growth hinged on the logging boom and the Minnesota and International Railway, which hauled logs to sawmills.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/funkley_minnesota",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1953, Funkley's entire population flew to New York City for a cancer bandage drive, meeting celebrities and the president.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/funkley_minnesota",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Funkley's landscape is shaped by glacial outwash plains, giving it sandy, gravelly soils typical of Beltrami County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/funkley_minnesota",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Funkley's post office operated from 1903 until 1967, after which services moved to Blackduck.",
